Let GAMESCOPE_IGNORE_STEAM_MODE_CONTROL give the Xwayland mode to the window.

In Gaming Mode the Steam client asserts a resolution of its own through
the GAMESCOPE_XWAYLAND_MODE_CONTROL root property, and re-asserts it
whenever the mode changes underneath it. On a Deck that is right: the
panel is fixed and Steam owns it. In a resizable nested window it fights
the window manager, and the window wins the frame while Steam wins the
resolution -- so dragging the edge only scales a stale buffer.

Dropping those requests leaves the game's Xwayland with nobody driving
it. steamcompmgr pushes the output size to server 0 alone, so Steam's own
display follows the window while the game's keeps the mode it was created
with: -W/-H, at the 60Hz fallback, since the host's real rate only
arrives with the first wp_presentation_feedback, which is after the
servers exist. A game then runs 1920x1080 at 60 inside a 144Hz window no
matter how large that window is. So the second hunk points every server
at the output, not just the first.

Not sent upstream: gating on an environment variable suits one machine,
upstream would want this driven by the backend reporting whether its
output size is user-controlled.

--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -6066,9 +6066,19 @@
 			{
 				bool root_size_identical = server->ctx->root_width == width && server->ctx->root_height == height;
 
-				wlserver_lock();
-				wlserver_set_xwayland_server_mode( server_idx, width, height, g_nOutputRefresh );
-				wlserver_unlock();
+				if ( getenv( "GAMESCOPE_IGNORE_STEAM_MODE_CONTROL" ) )
+				{
+					// Steam owns the mode on a Deck. In a resizable nested
+					// window the user owns it, so drop the request and still
+					// clear the property so Steam does not keep retrying.
+					root_size_identical = true;
+				}
+				else
+				{
+					wlserver_lock();
+					wlserver_set_xwayland_server_mode( server_idx, width, height, g_nOutputRefresh );
+					wlserver_unlock();
+				}
 
 				if ( root_size_identical )
 				{
@@ -8554,11 +8564,28 @@
 		{
 			if ( g_nXWaylandCount > 1 )
 			{
-				g_nNestedHeight = ( g_nNestedWidth * g_nOutputHeight ) / g_nOutputWidth;
-				wlserver_lock();
-				// Update only Steam, the root ctx, with the new output size for now
-				wlserver_set_xwayland_server_mode( 0, g_nOutputWidth, g_nOutputHeight, g_nOutputRefresh );
-				wlserver_unlock();
+				if ( getenv( "GAMESCOPE_IGNORE_STEAM_MODE_CONTROL" ) )
+				{
+					// Steam's requests are dropped above, and they were the only
+					// thing that ever moved the game's Xwayland off the size and
+					// rate it was born with. Hand every server the window, so a
+					// game sees the display the Deck UI already sees.
+					g_nNestedWidth = g_nOutputWidth;
+					g_nNestedHeight = g_nOutputHeight;
+
+					wlserver_lock();
+					for ( size_t i = 0; wlserver_get_xwayland_server( i ); i++ )
+						wlserver_set_xwayland_server_mode( i, g_nOutputWidth, g_nOutputHeight, g_nOutputRefresh );
+					wlserver_unlock();
+				}
+				else
+				{
+					g_nNestedHeight = ( g_nNestedWidth * g_nOutputHeight ) / g_nOutputWidth;
+					wlserver_lock();
+					// Update only Steam, the root ctx, with the new output size for now
+					wlserver_set_xwayland_server_mode( 0, g_nOutputWidth, g_nOutputHeight, g_nOutputRefresh );
+					wlserver_unlock();
+				}
 			}
 
